    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\nThe Abyss Park Tiny Is an All-Black, Super Fancy Take on Mobile Living in Two\n52 photos\nPhoto: LA Manufactured Homes LLC\nWho says a tiny house or a mobile home has to be cramped or even uncomfortable, lacking proper features and the kind of functionality that would make it suitable for everyday use? Well, whoever sees that needs to have a look at any park tiny out there, and this one in particular.\nThis is the APS 520MS from U.S.-based manufacturer Champion Homes. It's a couple's mobile home imagined as the perfect lake-front destination for an extended vacation, the entire summer, or even full-time living where permitted.\nLike most park tinies being made in the U.S., this one, too, can be customized to a large extent, from the finishes to the appliances, fabrics and colors, with the only invariable being the layout itself. You can see several options in the photos in the gallery, from a more colorful unit to one with modern touches throughout.\nYet the iteration of the APS 520M that still stands out, even two years after it emerged on the market, is the all-black variation dealers called The Abyss. It's dramatic, it has a strong personality, and it's designed to make an impression, but it also serves as a great example of different this floorplan is from all the others out there.\nPhoto: Champion Homes This park tiny may be imagined as a couple's home, but it's still big enough to fall in the category of Park RVs. That means that, while still mobile in every sense of the word, it's designed to stay put on location after initial delivery, which will have to take place with a special towing rig and special permits due to its dimensions.\nIt's 35 feet (10.6 meters) long overall and 15 feet (4.6 meters) wide, and offers 399 square feet (37 square meters) of living space, which, when employed for the comfort of just two occupants, feels comparatively huge.\nIn other words, when manufacturers aren't pressed to fit in accommodation for more people or more features for the higher number of occupants, 399 square feet is enough space to make a proper, sizable, and super fancy home. Like The Abyss here.\nPhoto: Champion Homes The other thing that makes this park tiny different from the others is the way in which it strives to create the feeling of open, flowing spaces while retaining the possibility of privacy. It does that by adding multiple entries into the same space, which works wonderfully with the extra-high ceilings to create the impression of much larger volumes.\nThe home sits on a quad axle custom trailer and features as highlight a wraparound porch designed as an outdoor entertainment area. That means it has ample space for a table and seats or whatever outdoor furniture you prefer, as well as a large fireplace with a wood wall that will accept a large-screen TV.\nThe porch also offers access inside through two different points. There's the main door in front of the house and then there's a side entryway into the bedroom – the only one inside. This being a couple's home, it's a one-bed, one-bad, no-loft floorplan, which means just about every feature you can think of is residential in size.\nPhoto: LA Manufactured Homes LLC The layout puts the living room at the front of the trailer, the kitchen in the middle section of the trailer, and the only bedroom and bathroom at the back, sharing the width of the trailer .\nThe living room is framed by large windows on three sides, so it's always basked in natural light. As shown in both video and photos, it's large enough to fit a sofa and maybe a few other pieces of furniture, and will flow directly into the kitchen space with no direct separation.\nThe kitchen is impressive for any type of mobile home. It's a two-block unit with residential, extra-large appliances (including a dishwasher, which is now a staple in this type of park tinies), plenty of cabinetry for storage, plenty of counter space, and – again – plenty of space to move around.\nPhoto: Champion Homes Finishes are up to each customer , from the color of the cabinets to the backsplashes and counters, but The Abyss lives up to its name with all-black everything: cabinetry, appliances, and tiling. Even the sink, a double-basin one, is black in this one, which might not be the most practical color but it sure is striking.\nThe bathroom is technically a pass-through one, in that you can access it both from the bedroom and the little hallway outside the kitchen space, where you have space for a stackable washer and dryer. It's also a full bathroom, with a walk-in shower with glass doors, a plumbed toilet, and a sink on a vanity that you can choose to have matched to the kitchen.\nThe bedroom is also accessible from several points – three to be more exact. You can enter it from the bathroom, from the same hallway with the washer-dryer, or from the porch. If the two people choosing this park tiny as their new home are into playing hide-and-seek, they won't be disappointed .\nPhoto: Champion Homes Also standard in this home is a full wardrobe behind barn doors and a dresser of drawers in the middle. Champion Homes doesn't say anything about the size of the bed that would go in there, but it should probably be a queen if you still want to have walkaround space.\nThe APS 520MS comes standard with two mini-splits for AC and heat, a water heater, and RV-style hookups for water, electricity, and sewage.\nPhoto: Champion Homes Pricing for each unit depends on the local retailer (Champion Homes doesn't offer any kind of figure for their homes) and the finishes you choose. Either way, you're likely looking at a $110,000+ purchase.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntiny house park tiny mobile home Champion Homes downsizing US WEEKEND\nAbout the author: Elena Gorgan\nElena has been writing for a living since 2006 and, as a journalist, she has put her double major in English and Spanish to good use. She covers automotive and mobility topics like cars and bicycles, and she always knows the shows worth watching on Netflix and friends. Full profile\nUP NEXT Spacecraft Reaches Asteroid NASA Hit Hard in 2022 for the First-Ever Space Crash Site Investigation",
